A||||15||fiero56||Question for oil experts re: mixing weights||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||11-18-2001||05:06 PM||||My Formula has 183K original miles on the engine. The engine has never been opened up. Once warmed, at idle oil pressure is very low, and I am running 10/40 right now. <P>I am wondering about the possibility of mixing a little 20/50 in with the 10/40. I have two major concerns about this. First is incompatibility of the oils due to weight. Second is the engine itself. Right now cold oil pressure at startup is 60psi. I am afraid if the pressure raises to 70-80 at cold start-up with thicker oil, that something will let go. Something like the timing chain, drive gear, pump input shaft, the pump itself, etc.<P>I have a 2.8 buld in the works as I type this, but it is still a good month away, probably closer to two. I also have a backup car my '86SE with light rod knock, so I'm not SOL if the Formula lets go with heavier oil.<P>Any warnings/comments/input about 3.5qts 10/40, and 1qt 20/50?||||reg||5
Z||000001||lowCG||11-18-2001||05:14 PM||||Before I got rid of it,my 2.8 was happy with Kendall 20/50,great stuff for high mile,or race-clearanced motors..||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||11-18-2001||07:02 PM||||Mobil1 5w-50 Full Synthetic.<P>Accept no substitutes.<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000003||jelly2m8||11-18-2001||07:11 PM||||I have a customer who has been using 20W50 in his 86 GT for the past 7 years. He drives this car in winter conditions, and hasent had any engine troubles thus far.<P> Do not put synthetic oils of any kind in your new motor when it gets built! Put some miles on it before you switch to synthetic. Most people put 8-10k miles on a new engine before switching to synthetics.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||11-18-2001||07:41 PM||||Would there be any ill effects of mixing 20/50 with 10/40? I don't feel comfortable running straight 20/50 on such worn out pieces, but 10/40 is beginnig to be too light(I remember saying that about 5/30 around 20K miles ago), and I think 20/50 might be too heavy, particularly when the temps will be hitting the teens soon.||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT GT 87||11-18-2001||08:39 PM||||My present car had bad rod bearings before I changed them . I run Mobil 15-50 all year even winter with no ploblems , make sure you use a GM  PF-52 oil filter !!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||11-18-2001||08:43 PM||||Oil is oil, mixng different viscosities has no ill effects. 10/40 + 20/50 = 15/45 oil.  An engine with 185K miles on it is probably ready for a rebuild.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||11-18-2001||09:01 PM||||Thanks Old Lar. I was thikning the same thing, but didn't want to do something stupid if I could avoid it. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>This engine won't be around much longer. I hope to pull it in another month or two. But in the meantime, its in my favorite car, which makes it my daily driver <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I'll pick some 20/50 up tommorrow.<P>With this many miles, I don't bother with synthetic, but I always run the Mobil1 oil filters in all my cars. They are the best I have found yet.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ITB 2M4||11-19-2001||12:24 AM||||Using synthetic on an engine with that many miles on it would be a waste of money.  The synthetic would actually find little cracks or gaps in gaskets and rings, and would disappear quickly out the exhaust or down the side of the block.<P>When you say the oil pressure is low, how low?  Lower than 25psi?  If it's above that, I wouldn't worry too much.  Below that, and there's probably other problems.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000009||Nashco||11-19-2001||12:51 AM||||25 psi? LOL..I'm willing to bet his oil pressure with that many miles is hanging around 10 psi warm at idle.  My car has 160k miles and I use 10/40 (cold, summer I use 20/50) because it's worn out.  Warmed up and idling, I get probably 8-12 psi oil pressure.  Cold at idle I've got about 60, but as the engine warms up, the tolerances start to show with the thinner oil.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||camon||11-19-2001||02:21 AM||||Well acording to my 87 GT owners manual....<P>Manual Quote........<BR>"Under normal engine operating conditions, the oil pressure should be 20-60 psi. Should the oil pressure drop below this (other then at idle)immediatly turn engine off and investigate the cause. Slight flucuations in oil pressure gage may occur; this is normal. Oil pressure should not fall below 4psi even at idle. Pressures are always higher when engine is cold."<P>That is a word for word quote from my 87 GT owners manual!<P>I have been running with somewhat low pressure ever since I got my fiero (about 2 or 3 months now) and it hangs right around 18-20psi (right on redline). This also concerned me but then I read the owners manual and if GM says it's ok who am I to question them. With that said.........<BR>I guess I could try to put on a high volume oil pump. But I feel so intimidated about dropping the oil pan to replace the pump (sounds like a job and a half)!<P>I guess I could try running a thicker oil (10/40 or something). I curently am using 10/30!<P>Just wanted to comment on this topic!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000011||tgowens||11-19-2001||03:04 AM||||Drew, go ahead and add some 20/50 - just add it to the 10/40, don't make a complete change though ... <P>Also, I may get back there for Christmas if you need any help on your 2.8 rebuild...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Nashco||11-19-2001||03:28 AM||||Any engine will have lower oil pressure after being worn out.  This is because the oil pump is pushing oil through the bearing areas, along with others.  As the oil pump gears wear out, and the volume between the crank and the bearings (due to bearing wear) increases, the oil pressure at idle decreases.  This usually doesn't surpass the oil pumps ability to pump until you have a lot of wear on the engine.<P>As a general rule, an engine will survive with 1 psi oil pressure for every 100 rpms.  This is minimum though, after this, you're asking for it.<P>A high volume oil pump is only going to prolong the agony.  If you're going to be dropping the pan, you should also replace the crank and rod bearings at the same time.  This "temporary" fix may prolong engine life another 20 or 30 thousand miles...usually until your piston rings wear out, which depends on how the engine's been treated.<P>You'd be much better off buying a rebuildable core longblock at the junkyard if your engine is worn out.  Get the cylinders honed, new bearings, piston rings, oil pump, valves reground, and head and block checked for flatness and magnafluxed.  Gasket kit, new water pump, and some spray paint.  Under a grand, and you'll have a pretty new engine <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||11-19-2001||08:17 AM||||Terry and everyone else, thank-you.<P>I don't need synthetic to find leaks to the "outside". My dyno oil does a nice job at that already  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>The bottom end is nice and loose on this engine. Warm oil pressure at idle is just a tick north of the red with 10/40. I realize 183K is well broken in on most any engine(particularly the 60* v-6). At hot idle the bottom end likes to let me know it is loose. It doesn't knock, but it definitely makes a little noise from time to time. <P>I'll add some 20/50 today. I finally got a garage, so it is hibernating right now, I drove the SE.<P>Terry, if I run into anything, I will let you know <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I will be out of town at the end of the month though.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000014||camon||11-19-2001||12:54 PM||||Nashco:<P>Thanks for the input......<P>I don't know how worn out the engine is. It has about 110k on it and seeing I have only had it about 3 to 4 months and it had 109k on it when I got it, i'm not sure how well it was taken care of mechanicaly.<P>I know it seems to have plenty of power (comparing it to my old 86 SE V6 which when I got it...had like 20k....which I don't have it anymore). <P>I have checked cylinder compression on my 87 GT and I get about 13-15psi in all 6 cylinders....so the engine seems strong yet.<P>The low oil pressure I was thinking maybe due to a gunked up screen or an oil pump that is showing age. If that is the case and I go through the hassle of dropping the pan and replacing the pump I thought I would just spend the extra 30 bucks for the High Volume pump. I guess it would also be a good idea to replace the bearing and crank while in there as well.<P>Question.....<BR>Can the bearings and crank be replaced fairly easily by just dropping the pan (I've never messed with this kind of stuff but if fairly easy to do I will give it a go when I replace the pump).<P>Thanks again!!!!<P>------------------<BR>Keith<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000015||rogergarrison||11-19-2001||04:13 PM||||any oil pressure above the red is acceptable at idle. I had a Lincoln that ran between 5 & 10 pnds at idle for 100,000 miles. Anything above 30-40 driving is still OK. I use 5w50 Castrol in mine year round (ohio).||||reg||1
